\section{Methodology}

We design a controlled iterative self-training experiment to isolate how embedding-based diversity selection interacts with model collapse. The experiment holds the training protocol, data budget, and selection algorithm constant while varying only the source of embeddings used for data selection, enabling direct attribution of observed differences to the embedding alignment gap.

\subsection{Base Model and Dataset}

We use GPT2-125M (124M parameters) as our base model, loaded from the HuggingFace pretrained checkpoint. We train and evaluate on WikiText-103, which contains 232,588 training samples of long-form Wikipedia articles.

We choose GPT2-125M for two reasons. First, its modest size permits running full self-training pipelines across multiple generations, data selection methods, and random seeds---a combinatorial design that would be prohibitively expensive with larger models. Second, despite its size, GPT2-125M exhibits the representation learning dynamics characteristic of transformer architectures, including the embedding drift and dimensional collapse phenomena central to our analysis \citep{jing2022understanding}.

\subsection{Self-Training Protocol}

We implement iterative self-training in a student-as-generator loop: at each generation, the current model generates synthetic text, a data selection method curates a subset, and the model is fine-tuned on that subset to produce the next-generation model.

We run 8 generations (Generation 0 through 7), all starting from the same pretrained GPT2-125M checkpoint. Each generation proceeds as follows: (1)~the model generates 500K tokens via nucleus sampling (temperature $0.8$, top-$p$ $0.95$), conditioned on prefix prompts drawn from WikiText-103; (2)~a data selection method selects a subset (or, for Uncurated, retains all data); (3)~the model is fine-tuned on the selected data starting from the previous generation's checkpoint (cumulative fine-tuning). Generation~0 uses the pretrained checkpoint as its starting point; methods diverge from Generation~0 onward because each applies its own selection criterion to the same generated pool.

All generations share identical training hyperparameters: learning rate $5 \times 10^{-5}$ with linear warmup, batch size 4 with gradient accumulation over 8 steps (effective batch size 32), maximum sequence length 512, and 3 training epochs per generation.

\subsection{Data Selection Methods}

We compare four data selection methods designed as a controlled set of ablations. Three methods select 50\% of the generated data; the fourth uses all of it.

\paragraph{Uncurated (100\%).} All generated data is used without any selection. This baseline captures the natural trajectory of model collapse under iterative self-training \citep{shumailov2024ai, alemohammad2024self}.

\paragraph{RGDS (50\%).} Representation-Guided Diversity Selection uses the training model's own last-hidden-layer embeddings to select a maximally diverse data subset. We first project the 768-dimensional embeddings to 32 dimensions via PCA fitted on the WikiText-103 reference set, reducing computational cost and removing noisy dimensions. We then apply a greedy selection algorithm that minimizes the \emph{normalized eigenvalue variance} of the selected set's covariance matrix, $\lVert \mathbf{C}(S) \rVert_F / \operatorname{tr}(\mathbf{C}(S))^2$, which favors subsets whose embedding covariance has uniform eigenvalues---i.e., maximal dimensional coverage. At each step, the candidate whose inclusion most reduces this objective is added to the selected set. The core assumption is that diversity in the model's embedding space reflects diversity in content.

\paragraph{External-only (50\%).} This method applies the same greedy diversity selection algorithm and PCA projection but uses embeddings from a frozen Contriever encoder \citep{izacard2022unsupervised} instead of the training model's own embeddings. Contriever's parameters are fixed throughout all generations and are never updated during self-training. This isolates a single variable: by holding the selection algorithm constant, any performance difference between RGDS and External-only is attributable to whether the embedding space co-evolves with the model.

\paragraph{Random 50\%.} Samples are selected uniformly at random to fill the 50\% budget. This provides the cleanest ablation for disentangling selection quality from data budget effects.

\paragraph{Controlled comparisons.} The four methods yield a factorial ablation structure:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{RGDS vs.\ Random 50\%}: identical data budget; differences arise solely from selection mechanism quality.
    \item \textbf{RGDS vs.\ External-only}: identical budget and algorithm; differences arise from the embedding source (drifting internal vs.\ frozen external).
    \item \textbf{Random 50\% vs.\ Uncurated}: isolates the effect of data budget (50\% vs.\ 100\%) without any intelligent selection.
    \item \textbf{External-only vs.\ Random 50\%}: quantifies the gain of embedding-guided selection when embeddings are stable.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Selection Ratio}

To diagnose \emph{what} each selection method preferentially retains, we define the selection ratio, a per-generation metric that measures whether the selected subset is enriched or depleted in content diversity relative to the full generated pool:

\begin{equation}
    \text{SR}_g = \frac{\bar{r}(\mathcal{S}_g)}{\bar{r}(\mathcal{D}_g)}
\end{equation}

\noindent where $\bar{r}(\mathcal{S}_g)$ is the mean rare n-gram density of the selected subset $\mathcal{S}_g$ at generation $g$, and $\bar{r}(\mathcal{D}_g)$ is the mean rare n-gram density of the full generated pool $\mathcal{D}_g$. We define a rare n-gram as any trigram whose count falls at or below the 10th percentile of the trigram frequency distribution in the WikiText-103 training set, yielding a data-adaptive threshold.

The selection ratio admits a direct interpretation:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\text{SR} > 1$: the method preferentially retains samples rich in rare n-grams, aligning selection with content diversity.
    \item $\text{SR} < 1$: the method preferentially retains samples poor in rare n-grams, anti-aligning selection with content diversity.
    \item $\text{SR} \approx 1$: selection is uncorrelated with content diversity (the expected value under uniform random selection).
\end{itemize}

\noindent Tracking $\text{SR}_g$ across generations reveals whether a selection method's relationship to content diversity is stable or shifts as the model degrades.

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}

We evaluate each method at every generation along two axes: language modeling quality and diversity.

\paragraph{Language modeling quality.} We report perplexity (PPL) on the WikiText-103 validation set.

\paragraph{Lexical diversity.} We measure the rare n-gram ratio---the proportion of trigrams in generated text that qualify as rare under the definition above---and Distinct-$n$ \citep{li2016diversity}, the fraction of unique $n$-grams ($n = 1, 2, 3$) in generated text.

\paragraph{Generation diversity.} We compute Self-BLEU \citep{zhu2018texygen}: the average BLEU score between pairs of generated samples. Lower Self-BLEU indicates greater diversity across generations.

\paragraph{Representation quality.} We track two embedding-space diagnostics. RankMe measures the effective rank of the embedding matrix via singular value entropy \citep{garrido2023rankme}: a higher RankMe indicates more uniform utilization of embedding dimensions. The $k$-NN probe accuracy measures how well embeddings separate content categories via $k$-nearest-neighbor classification: declining accuracy signals loss of discriminative structure.

\subsection{Reproducibility}

All experiments are repeated across 3 random seeds (42, 123, 456). Each seed determines the random initialization of fine-tuning, the sampling randomness during generation, and (for Random 50\%) the subset selection. All methods within a given seed start from the same pretrained checkpoint and, at Generation~0, the same generated data pool, so that observed differences are attributable to the selection method alone. We report means and standard deviations across seeds.

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

Table~\ref{tab:gen7_summary} summarizes Generation~7 metrics across all methods and seeds. Figure~\ref{fig:ppl_trends} shows per-generation perplexity trajectories. We organize findings around three primary claims and one directional observation.

\subsection{RGDS Produces Catastrophic Perplexity Degradation}
\label{sec:result_c1}

RGDS is the only method that exhibits catastrophic performance degradation across generations. At Generation~7, RGDS reaches a perplexity of $78.32 \pm 4.60$ ($n{=}2$\textsuperscript{$\dagger$}), compared to $47.04 \pm 2.90$ for Uncurated ($n{=}3$; +66\%) and $43.18 \pm 1.43$ for Random~50\% ($n{=}3$; +82\%). Both completed seeds show the same pattern: 75.07 (seed=42) and 81.57 (seed=123), with effect sizes that exceed inter-seed variance within any other method by an order of magnitude.

The selection ratio traces the mechanism behind this degradation. RGDS's selection ratio follows an overall declining trend from ${\sim}1.04$ at Generation~0 to $0.52 \pm 0.004$ at Generation~7, with a transient rebound during Generations~1--3 ($0.91 \to 0.93 \to 0.94$) before sustained decline from Generation~4 onward. By the final generation, RGDS preferentially retains vocabulary-poor samples: its selected subsets contain approximately half the rare trigram density of the full generated pool. The model's internal notion of ``diverse'' has inverted relative to content diversity.

% TODO: seed=456 RGDS 完成后更新为 n=3 mean±std 并补充统计检验 p 值

\subsection{External Embeddings Avoid the Alignment Gap}
\label{sec:result_c2}

External-only selection applies the identical selection algorithm and 50\% data budget but uses frozen Contriever embeddings. It avoids catastrophic degradation entirely: its Generation~7 perplexity of $47.59 \pm 2.93$ ($n{=}2$\textsuperscript{$\dagger$}) falls within the range of Uncurated ($47.04 \pm 2.90$) and Random~50\% ($43.18 \pm 1.43$).

External-only maintains a selection ratio above~1.0 throughout all generations ($1.11 \pm 0.02$ at Generation~7), indicating continued preferential retention of content-diverse samples. Its rank correlation between diversity scores and rare token content remains weakly positive ($+0.011 \pm 0.000$; Table~\ref{tab:gen7_summary}), while RGDS's correlation turns negative ($-0.035 \pm 0.005$).

Since RGDS and External-only differ in exactly one variable---the embedding source---the 30.7-point perplexity gap at Generation~7 ($78.32$ vs.\ $47.59$) is directly attributable to the use of co-degrading internal representations. The selection algorithm is not at fault; the failure is specific to coupling data selection to embeddings that co-evolve with the collapsing model.

% TODO: seed=456 ext/rgds 完成后更新为 n=3 mean±std 并补充 RGDS vs External t 检验

\subsection{Internal Selection Underperforms Random Baseline}
\label{sec:result_c3}

The comparison between RGDS and Random~50\% provides the cleanest controlled ablation: both use the same 50\% data budget, isolating the effect of the selection mechanism itself. RGDS's Generation~7 perplexity ($78.32 \pm 4.60$, $n{=}2$\textsuperscript{$\dagger$}) exceeds Random~50\% ($43.18 \pm 1.43$, $n{=}3$) by 82\%. Internal embedding diversity selection does not merely fail to improve upon random sampling---it actively degrades model quality relative to uninformed selection.

Rare token ratio confirms this finding: RGDS retains $0.0057 \pm 0.0008$ at Generation~7, compared to $0.0098 \pm 0.0003$ for Random~50\%---a 42\% reduction in lexical tail content.

\subsection{Random Pruning: A Directional Observation}
\label{sec:result_s3}

Random~50\% ($43.18 \pm 1.43$, $n{=}3$) achieves lower Generation~7 perplexity than Uncurated ($47.04 \pm 2.90$, $n{=}3$), a reduction of 8.2\%. The direction is consistent across all three seeds. However, this difference does not reach conventional significance ($p \approx 0.10$, two-sided $t$-test), and we report it as a directional observation rather than a confirmed finding.

If confirmed with additional statistical power, this result would suggest that training on a random 50\% subset provides mild regularization compared to using all generated data in the iterative self-training setting, consistent with observations that data pruning can improve generalization even without informed selection \citep{sorscher2022beyond}.

While the perplexity difference remains directional, Random~50\% shows a statistically significant advantage in lexical diversity: its Generation~7 rare n-gram ratio ($0.0098 \pm 0.0003$) exceeds Uncurated ($0.0090 \pm 0.0004$) with $p = 0.025$ (two-sided $t$-test, $n{=}3$). Even uninformed subsampling preserves content diversity better than retaining all generated data, suggesting that the diversity benefit of curation does not require intelligent selection---merely reducing the volume of self-generated training data slows lexical tail erosion.

\subsection{Lexical Diversity Collapse Across Methods}
\label{sec:result_diversity}

RGDS exhibits the sharpest decline in rare token ratio, dropping from approximately 0.010 at Generation~0 to $0.0057 \pm 0.0008$ at Generation~7 (Table~\ref{tab:gen7_summary}). The remaining methods converge to a narrower band: Random~50\% ($0.0098 \pm 0.0003$), Uncurated ($0.0090 \pm 0.0004$), and External-only ($0.0102 \pm 0.0004$). Relative to the WikiText-103 baseline rare token ratio of approximately 0.029, all non-RGDS methods exhibit roughly 65\% lexical tail erosion by Generation~7; RGDS reaches approximately 80\%.

Unlike the perplexity-level normal cluster (ANOVA $p = 0.219$; \S\ref{sec:result_cluster}), the rare n-gram differences among non-RGDS methods are statistically significant (ANOVA $p = 0.007$, $n{=}3$ per method). In particular, Uncurated retains significantly fewer rare n-grams than Random~50\% ($p = 0.025$, two-sided $t$-test). Lexical diversity collapse is primarily driven by the iterative self-training loop itself \citep{shumailov2024ai}, but the degree of erosion is modulated by data selection: even uninformed curation (random subsampling) provides measurable protection relative to using all generated data. RGDS is distinctive in accelerating this inherent degradation far beyond all other methods.

\subsection{Representation Metrics Do Not Track Content Collapse}
\label{sec:result_rankme}

RankMe values at Generation~7 are comparable across all methods (289--296; Table~\ref{tab:gen7_summary}), with RGDS showing a slightly elevated value ($295.54 \pm 3.98$) relative to the other methods (${\sim}290$). The metric designed to track effective embedding dimensionality does not distinguish between catastrophically degraded RGDS and the normally performing alternatives, indicating that embedding structural properties can persist even as the space loses content-discriminative fidelity.

\subsection{Scaling to GPT2-Medium (355M)}
\label{sec:result_scaling}

Preliminary results from GPT2-Medium (355M) confirm that model collapse generalizes to larger models and is substantially amplified (Table~\ref{tab:medium_uncurated}). The Uncurated baseline perplexity increases by 245\% over 8 generations (26.55 to 91.66, seed=42, $n{=}1$), compared to 28\% for GPT2-125M (36.7 to 47.04, $n{=}3$). RankMe inflation increases by $+$72.4 points (vs.\ $+$11.1 for GPT2-125M), and the rank correlation between embedding diversity scores and rare token content turns negative at Generation~4, following the same trajectory observed in the smaller model.

The amplified baseline degradation at 355M parameters suggests that the embedding alignment gap may produce even more severe outcomes when RGDS is applied at this scale: if the Uncurated floor itself is worse, the additional degradation introduced by co-degrading internal embeddings could compound further. \placeholder{GPT2-Medium RGDS Generation~7 perplexity and selection ratio to confirm or disconfirm this prediction.}

